+## Scenario: the sponsor has a residence permit
+
+You are joining a spouse who holds a German residence permit. Start by recording the permit category exactly as the sponsor can document it, then use the competent mission’s instructions for that route. BAMF materials say basic German is generally relevant in certain spousal-reunification situations, while also describing possible different rules and boundaries. That is not a finding that you personally need a certificate or meet an exception. Preserve the mission page and ask it to confirm the requirement and accepted proof for the route shown in your application.
+
+## Scenario: the sponsor is a German national
+
+You are joining a German spouse from outside Germany. The Foreign Office describes applying at the competent German mission abroad, which forwards the application to the foreigners authority. Use the mission’s current page to discover its document process; do not borrow a checklist from a mission in another country. If the page points you to BAMF’s general information, treat it as orientation and keep the mission as the final source for your application instructions.
+
+For this bounded route, apply through the competent German mission abroad; that mission forwards the application to the foreigners authority. The responsible German mission or competent immigration authority decides the visa route and its document instructions. A selected exam centre controls only its current booking and test-day process. Those three sentences describe the ownership boundary; they do not determine an individual application.
