+
+## Who this is for
+
+Use this page when you are preparing a German naturalisation file and need to separate a language-evidence question from every other condition. It is especially useful if you have seen “B1” in a national-law summary and need to know what to ask the authority next. It is not a naturalisation assessment, a substitute for a current local checklist, or an opinion on an exception.
+
+National law, the local nationality authority, an exam owner and a test centre have different jobs. The Nationality Act is the source for the general legal framework. The competent authority assesses your individual application and local procedure. BAMF, Goethe or telc can explain their own tests. A centre can explain its current local administration. None of the latter three decides whether your complete naturalisation file is accepted.
+
+## Key decisions
+
+Under Section 10, the statutory language and civic-knowledge conditions are separate. The current text says that the language condition in paragraph 1 sentence 1 number 6 is met where the requirements of a CEFR B1 language examination are fulfilled, while the statute separately lists knowledge of Germany's legal and social order and living conditions. The provision also contains case-specific rules. Read this as a national-law starting point, not as a personal finding or a universal document list.
+
+Your local authority must tell you what it currently accepts as evidence, in which format, and how it is to be submitted. Ask separately about civic-knowledge evidence, application forms, identity documents and the local channel. A federal portal can orient you to the process; it does not settle a local document dispute or decide an individual exception.
+
+Only once the authority has identified possible language documents should you compare exam products. A product's CEFR label establishes what the provider says its product is, not that a nationality authority accepts that product in every form. Confirm the full certificate, any alternative evidence and the relevant date with the receiving authority before booking.
+
+### Scenario: the authority page says B1 and “Einbürgerungstest”
+
+Treat these as two questions. Put language evidence in one folder and civic-knowledge evidence in another. Ask the authority whether the terms on its page refer to your route and which documents it wants. Do not infer that one successful test creates the other evidence category. Keep the page URL and date because the local procedure may change.
+
+### Scenario: you completed an integration course years ago
+
+The past document may be important, but this page cannot decide its use today. Send the authority the exact certificate name and ask whether it is accepted for your file and whether it needs an original, copy, translation or other verification. Do not book another exam simply because a comparison chart claims a certificate has a universal validity period.
+
+### Original asset: citizenship four-owner evidence map and two-folder label
+
+Create a four-owner evidence map with columns for national law, competent nationality authority, exam owner and selected centre, then make a Language folder and a Civic knowledge folder. On every item write document title, issuing body, issue date, authority instruction it answers, format held and authority confirmation date. Keep a third folder called Wider file for every other statutory and local condition. This makes a missing category visible before submission.
+
+### Original asset: authority-question checklist
+
+Ask: “Which route and current local instruction apply? Which language documents are accepted in what format? Is civic-knowledge proof separately required and which documents are accepted? Which current form and submission channel should I use? What evidence is needed for any exception the authority says may be relevant?” Leave each answer blank until sourced.
+
+### Original asset: claim-boundary note
+
+For every note, add “supports” and “does not support.” Example: “Section 10 supports that the statute treats B1 language requirements and civic knowledge separately; it does not support that my local authority will accept my particular certificate.” The boundary line is a practical guard against over-reading a source.
+
+## Working sequence
+
+First identify the authority and current route information. Second, separate evidence categories before collecting documents. Third, ask the local authority to resolve document and procedure questions. Fourth, if an exam remains necessary, check the owner’s official product information and the selected centre’s local terms. Finally, compare the finished evidence folders to the authority’s current instruction before submitting. Never use a study plan, booking page or centre finder to manufacture an eligibility, exception or outcome.
+
+## Next action
+
+After the authority confirms possible language proof, continue to the [DTZ, Goethe B1 and telc B1 comparison](/guides/goethe-b1-vs-telc-b1/) for product boundaries. If civic knowledge is relevant, use the [Leben in Deutschland and language proof guide](/guides/germany-b1-leben-in-deutschland-and-language-proof/) to organise the two evidence categories without merging them.
+
+## Source boundaries and recheck
+
+The official Section 10 text supports the statutory wording it contains on the date you checked it. It cannot tell you that an authority has accepted your uploaded document, that an exception applies, or that every other application condition is met. A government naturalisation portal can orient you to the process, but it cannot replace the competent authority’s local instruction. Record that distinction in the evidence folder rather than relying on memory.
+
+For an exam page, write the issuer and product name exactly as published. This supports identification and preparation only. If a selected centre provides an appointment, payment or result-process statement, save its dated message with the centre name and exact product; do not extend it to other centres or dates. Where an answer is not written, ask the owner who can decide it. Obtain the authority’s response and compare it to the document you plan to use.
