-## Use this plan only after the route check
+## Who this is for
 
-This is a preparation plan, not a promise that 30 days is enough. Confirm that A1 applies to your case, the certificate is accepted, and the exam date is realistic before building your calendar around it.
+Use this plan if your route and certificate question are already being checked and you are preparing specifically for the adult **Goethe-Zertifikat A1: Start Deutsch 1**. It turns official training material and the absorbed listening, speaking, writing, working-adult and safe-resource guidance into a repeatable month. It does not promise that 30 days is enough, predict questions, replace a class, or decide whether a mission will accept a certificate.
 
-Goethe describes its adult A1 exam as listening, reading, writing, and speaking. The plan therefore keeps all four skills in view instead of letting one familiar skill take over the month.
+Goethe’s official exam and training pages distinguish the adult A1 exam from the young-people’s version and provide practice material for listening, reading, writing and speaking. Use those resources to calibrate the task format. Use the selected centre—not this plan—for current date, identity and test-day procedures.
 
-## Your minimum daily block
+## Key decisions
 
-Set aside 35 to 60 minutes on most days. Use one short listening task, one reading or vocabulary task, one small writing task, and one spoken response. Keep one notebook or note file for repeated errors.
+### Use one official format anchor
 
-If you only have 20 minutes, do listening plus speaking one day and reading plus writing the next. Consistency is more useful than a single long session followed by several missed days.
+Choose the current adult Goethe A1 training page as your format anchor. Download or open a permitted sample, identify the four skills, and create a small practice routine around it. Do not buy leaked questions, copy real answers, or treat a sample task as a forecast. Other legitimate study resources can help, but the official material tells you what format you are preparing for.
 
-## Days 1 to 7: make personal information reliable
+### Keep all four skills visible
 
-Work on the alphabet, numbers, greetings, name, address, country, city, phone number, time, and dates. Practise spelling these details aloud and filling them into a simple form.
+Each study day should touch two or more skills, and each week should revisit listening, reading, writing and speaking. A learner who only memorises a long introduction can miss simple information, spelling, form completion, or a short request. Keep responses small enough to review and repeat. The goal is controlled basic communication, not a polished script.
 
-By day 7, you should be able to introduce yourself briefly, answer basic personal questions, and notice which numbers or sounds still cause hesitation.
+### Adapt effort instead of inventing an outcome
 
-## Days 8 to 14: handle everyday exchanges
+Use a smaller daily block when work or care duties are heavy, then record what was actually completed. If repeated errors remain or the official practice format is unfamiliar, extend the plan or review the exam date with your own circumstances in mind. Neither the plan nor a practice score can promise an exam, certificate, visa or booking outcome.
 
-Use familiar areas such as family, work, shopping, food, appointments, travel, and housing. Read short notices, write simple form entries or messages, and practise asking for or giving basic information.
+## 30-day study board
 
-Keep answers short. The target is a usable exchange, not a memorised paragraph. Add the [speaking routine](/guides/goethe-a1-speaking-topics/) on at least four days this week.
+| Days | Focus | Daily evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 1–7 | personal details, alphabet, numbers, time and dates | one spoken introduction, one form entry |
+| 8–14 | everyday exchanges, notices and short messages | one question/answer pair, one short written response |
+| 15–21 | official-style four-skill practice | one timed sample block and error note |
+| 22–30 | repair weak skills and calm rehearsal | revised errors, centre-instruction check |
 
-## Days 15 to 21: connect skill practice to format
+For a normal day, choose a 35–60 minute block: short listening, reading/vocabulary, a few written lines, and one spoken response. With only 20 minutes, alternate listening/speaking with reading/writing; log the missed pair rather than pretending it happened.
 
-Use official practice material to understand the current task style. Work in short timed blocks: listen once without pausing, read a notice or message for its main point, complete a simple written task, then answer a spoken prompt aloud.
+### Scenario: you work full time and miss several evenings
 
-At the end of each session, write down one error you can repair tomorrow. Do not chase a new resource whenever a task feels difficult.
+Do not compensate with an unsustainable marathon. Restore the next small block, repeat one weak item, and keep the weekly review. A short recorded answer, simple form, and targeted listening task are useful evidence of practice. Move the calendar forward if necessary; do not shorten the route or invent readiness because an exam date feels urgent.
 
-## Days 22 to 30: repair and rehearse
+### Scenario: listening is much weaker than speaking
 
-Choose the two weakest areas from your notes. Spend the first part of the week repairing them, then combine all four skills in calm timed practice.
+Use an official audio task after reading the prompt, listen once without pausing where the material permits, then identify whether the gap was a number, time, place, instruction or attention. Add original drills for that category. Avoid looping a single answer until it is memorised. Pair the listening work with a one-minute spoken response so a familiar skill does not take over every study session.
 
-Use the final days to rehearse practical details too: your arrival plan, the exact identification required by the centre, booking confirmation, and a simple speaking warm-up. Check current local instructions rather than copying another candidate's test-day routine.
+### Scenario: you can speak but writing breaks down
 
-## When this plan is not enough
+Create short original forms and messages using changing names, dates, places and requested actions. Underline required details, write a first version without automatic translation, then check spelling, numbers and omissions. The task is not a predicted prompt. Use official training to understand the current format, then use your own variations to practise transferable control.
 
-Extend the plan when basic details are still difficult to understand, spell, or say; when timed work repeatedly breaks down; or when you have not yet used official practice material. More time is a planning choice, not a personal failure.
+## Four-skill error log
 
-If you are rebuilding after an unsuccessful result, use the [Goethe A1 retake guide](/guides/goethe-a1-retake-policy/) to choose the next date only after reviewing the score report and local policy.
+Use four headings: listening, reading, writing, speaking. After each block, add one error you can name and one repair task for tomorrow. Examples: “confused 15 and 50—number drill,” “missed a date—listen once and note dates,” “left out an instruction—underline task points,” or “answer too long—give one clear sentence.” Keep the log private and concise.
 
-## Weekly review checklist
+## Weekly checkpoint card
 
-- Can you answer simple personal questions without a long script?
-- Can you understand short everyday information at the practice level you are using?
-- Can you complete a simple form or short message carefully?
-- Can you ask for and respond to basic information aloud?
-- Is your next official action clear: study, book, confirm documents, or wait for a better date?
+At each week’s end, answer: Can I understand a short familiar message at the level I am practising? Can I provide basic personal details clearly? Can I write a short form or message with the required details? Can I ask and answer a simple question? Which two errors recur? What official or local action remains open? The card tells you what to practise next; it is not a pass prediction.
+
+## Mistakes to avoid
+
+- Treating 30 calendar days as an assurance of readiness.
+- Using youth material when preparing for the adult product.
+- Memorising leaked or copied “real questions.”
+- Spending every session on the skill that already feels comfortable.
+- Switching resources whenever an error appears instead of recording and repairing it.
+- Assuming a study plan answers local centre or mission questions.
 
 ## Next action
 
-Use [Goethe A1 speaking topics](/guides/goethe-a1-speaking-topics/) and [Goethe A1 listening practice](/guides/goethe-a1-listening-practice/) for targeted daily work. Before booking, return to the [Germany A1 family reunion hub](/germany-family-reunion-a1/) and verify the current local requirements.
+Open the [official Goethe A1 training](https://www.goethe.de/en/spr/prf/ueb/pa1.html), choose the first small block, and set up the four-skill log. Before the final week, return to the [German A1 documents checklist](/guides/german-a1-documents-checklist/) and [Goethe A1 test centers](/guides/goethe-a1-test-centers/) to recheck the selected centre’s current test-day instruction and the route’s official requirements.
+
+## Four-owner responsibility map
+
+Preparation uses four parties without blurring their authority. **National/orientation authority:** the Foreign Office can point a family-reunion reader toward the competent mission; it does not set a study outcome. **Competent mission or receiving authority:** controls the language-proof question for the application route. **Exam owner:** Goethe publishes the adult A1 product and official training material that anchors practice. **Selected centre and reader:** the centre supplies current local test-day administration, while the reader chooses lawful original practice, keeps a four-skill error log, and extends or reshapes the study board when evidence shows that more preparation is needed.
 
 ## Official sources
 
-- [Goethe-Institut German examinations](https://www.goethe.de/en/spr/prf.html)
-- [Official Goethe A1 exam training](https://www.goethe.de/en/spr/prf/ueb/pa1.html)
+- [Goethe German examinations](https://www.goethe.de/en/spr/prf.html) — checked 2026-09-01; supports the adult A1 product, four skills and the local-centre boundary.
+- [Goethe A1 exam training](https://www.goethe.de/en/spr/prf/ueb/pa1.html) — checked 2026-09-01; provides official adult practice material for listening, reading, writing and speaking.
+- [Goethe A1 adult exam page](https://www.goethe.de/ins/de/en/prf/prf/gzsd1.html) — checked 2026-09-01; supports the adult-product description and links readers to local dates and prices, not a global schedule.
+- [Federal Foreign Office language-skills FAQ](https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/visa-service/buergerservice/faq/01a-deutschkenntnisse-606682) — checked 2026-09-01; supports keeping the visa requirement question with the competent mission.
+
+## A sustainable monthly rhythm
+
+Treat the board as four short loops rather than a countdown. At the beginning of a week, choose one official sample or a small original exercise and identify the skill. During the week, practise the smallest repeatable unit: a time expression, a notice, a form field, a short request. At the end, review the error log and select only one or two repairs. This is particularly useful for working adults because an interrupted day does not invalidate the whole plan; it simply creates an item to resume.
+
+Keep preparation lawful and calm. Official sample material is useful because it provides a transparent format reference. Your own prompts, recordings and error notes create the repetition that a sample alone cannot provide. Avoid “insider” material, promised question lists, answer memorisation services and guaranteed-pass marketing. They do not give a dependable account of your current skills and can distract from basic listening, reading, writing and speaking practice.
+
+In the final days, spend a few minutes checking the real-world handoff: confirmed centre, exact exam, current test-day instruction and safe travel plan. Do not use the study board to answer an open immigration or booking question. Put that question back into the appropriate route, centre, timeline or document record, then return to focused practice.
