-## Direct practice answer
-
-Goethe B1 can feel difficult when several language skills have to work under exam conditions. The official exam has reading, listening, writing and speaking modules, so use current official training to identify which module needs repair.
-
-If you already have A2 basics, your best use of time is to identify the weakest module, practise with official format examples, and repair repeated mistakes instead of studying every textbook chapter again.
-
 ## Who this is for
 
-### Best for
-
-- Learners moving from A2 to B1.
-- Germany settlement, work, or long-term residence applicants checking whether Goethe B1 is realistic.
-- Students who can understand simple German but lose points in timed exam tasks.
-
-### Skip if
+Replace a vague difficulty rating with evidence from unfamiliar practice tasks across four skills. This page provides a working method, not an individual exam or application decision.
 
-- You have not confirmed that B1 is required for your route.
-- You are looking for copied real exam questions.
-- You need local fee or seat information before choosing a test center.
+## Key decisions
 
-## The A2-to-B1 jump
+Choose the exact product or application task first. Keep official requirements separate from the original practice or checking activities below.
 
-At A2, you can often survive with memorised phrases and familiar topics. At B1, you must handle longer input, infer meaning, explain opinions, and produce connected writing.
+## Separate familiarity from ability
 
-The most common jump is endurance. You may know enough German in short exercises but lose accuracy when several modules are tested on the same day.
+Use an unfamiliar permitted practice task before judging readiness. Note whether difficulty came from understanding the instructions, extracting information, organising a response or maintaining an interaction. A task repeated from memory does not give the same evidence.
 
-## Module-by-module difficulty
+## Make a four-row gap table
 
-| Module | Why it feels hard | Repair focus |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Reading | Longer texts contain distractors and implied meaning. | Practise under time limits and mark why each wrong option is wrong. |
-| Listening | Timed audio can make details easy to miss. | Drill numbers, dates, speaker attitude, and paraphrases. |
-| Writing | You must answer all prompt points in a clear structure. | Use a fixed opening, body, request, and closing pattern. |
-| Speaking | You must react to a partner and stay understandable. | Record short answers and practise repair phrases. |
+For listening, reading, writing and speaking, record one task attempted, the observed difficulty and the next repair. Keep objective answer checking separate from feedback on productive skills. Do not average unlike observations into a made-up pass probability.
 
-## Grammar and vocabulary load
+## Choose the next repair by impact
 
-B1 does not require perfect advanced grammar. It does require enough control to make your meaning clear. Prioritise:
+Start with an error that repeatedly prevents task completion: for example, answering a different question or omitting a requested point. Plan a short original exercise and then retry a fresh task. More vocabulary alone may not address an instruction or organisation problem.
 
-- Verb position in main and subordinate clauses.
-- Modal verbs and common past-tense forms.
-- Connectors for reason, contrast, sequence, and opinion.
-- Everyday vocabulary for work, health, housing, education, travel, media, and administration.
+## Reassess the schedule
 
-## Targeting the real difficulty
+Compare two separate practice attempts under recorded conditions. If the same obstacle remains, extend preparation or obtain targeted teaching support. This page cannot rate your personal chance of passing or decide how a receiving authority will use a certificate.
 
-Do one timed sample task per module, then create an error log. For every mistake, write:
+## Mistakes to avoid
 
-- The task type.
-- The reason for the mistake.
-- The fix you will practise.
-- The date you will retest it.
+Do not treat a sample, practice result or another person’s experience as confirmation of your own booking, certificate acceptance or readiness. Record unresolved questions rather than filling in dates, fees or outcomes.
 
-Retesting is the important part. A long mistake list is not useful unless it changes the next practice session.
-
-## Common misdiagnoses
-
-- "My grammar is bad" can actually mean poor writing structure.
-- "Listening is impossible" can mean weak number and date recognition.
-- "I need more vocabulary" can mean you are not using context clues.
-- "I failed speaking" can mean you had no repair phrases when you got stuck.
-
-## Goethe B1 vs telc B1 difficulty
+## Next action
 
-Do not choose between Goethe and telc by forum reputation. First confirm which exact proof the receiving authority accepts, then compare each provider's current official materials and local terms.
+Complete the working record above, then use [the related guide](/guides/goethe-b1-study-plan/) to continue. Reopen the official source for any current product, local or application requirement.
 
